"# Chapter 11 — Circular imports (there are none — but mutual recursion is real)

In a module-having Scheme, what happens when module A imports B and B imports A? That circular import is a recurring quiet bug in module systems everywhere. **Motoi has no imports, so it has no import cycles at all.** There is nothing to cycle: the standard library is loaded once, and your program is one body of top-level definitions.

But there is a real, adjacent thing worth teaching: two of your *own* verbs that call each other — **mutual recursion**. In an import system this would tempt you into a module cycle; in Motoi it is just two top-level `define`s, and it works cleanly because Motoi resolves names at call time.

## 11.1 The shape people reach for imports to solve

In the other dialect you might split two mutually-referencing verbs into two modules that import each other — `a` needs `bar` from `b`, `b` needs `foo` from `a` — and hit the cycle. The verbs really do reference each other:

- `foo` calls `bar`
- `bar` calls `foo`

In Motoi you do not split them across importing modules (there is no import). You just define both at the top level. A forward reference — `foo` mentioning `bar` before `bar` is defined — is fine, because the reference is resolved when `foo` is *called*, not when it is *defined*.

## 11.2 Mutual recursion, done plainly

Here is the honest Motoi version: two verbs that call each other, defined top-to-bottom, no cycle machinery, no warning, it just runs:

```scheme
(define (even? n)
  (if (= n 0) #t (odd? (- n 1))))

(define (odd? n)
  (if (= n 0) #f (even? (- n 1))))

(list (even? 10) (odd? 7))
;; ⇒ (#t #t)
```

`even?` names `odd?` before `odd?` exists in the file. That is not a cycle to detect and warn about — it is ordinary Scheme. By the time you *call* `even?`, `odd?` is defined, so the lookup succeeds.

## 11.3 When mutual recursion means something is off

The old advice about import cycles maps onto a real design question about mutually-recursive verbs. If two verbs lean on each other heavily, ask:

- **Do they belong together?** Often two mutually-recursive verbs are really one idea; keeping them side by side (as above) is correct, not a smell.
- **Should a shared helper hold the common part?** If both call the same sub-computation, factor it into a third verb both call.
- **Should one call become a value passed in?** Instead of `bar` hard-calling `foo`, have the caller pass `foo` (or a callback) to `bar`. This decouples them.

These are design choices, not cycle-breaking rituals — because Motoi imposes no ritual.

## 11.4 Keeping them together

If two verbs are genuinely one idea, keep them adjacent at the top level (section 11.2). There is nothing to merge and nothing to un-cycle. If you wanted them *private* to a single public verb, nest both in an inner scope:

```scheme
(define (parity n)
  (define (ev? k) (if (= k 0) #t (od? (- k 1))))
  (define (od? k) (if (= k 0) #f (ev? (- k 1))))
  (if (ev? n) 'even 'odd))

(list (parity 4) (parity 5))
;; ⇒ (even odd)
```

`ev?` and `od?` are mutually recursive *and* private to `parity`. Internal defines see each other, so the forward reference works here too.

## 11.5 The shared-helper refactor

If two verbs share a common step, factor it out — the Motoi version of the other dialect's \"shared third module\":

```scheme
(define (common-step x) (* x x))

(define (path-a x) (+ (common-step x) 1))
(define (path-b x) (- (common-step x) 1))

(list (path-a 3) (path-b 3))
;; ⇒ (10 8)
```

`path-a` and `path-b` do not call each other at all; both call `common-step`. No cycle, no coupling between the two paths.

## 11.6 The callback refactor

When you genuinely want one verb to call into behavior it does not name directly, pass the behavior in as a value — the other dialect's callback pattern, done with a plain lambda argument:

```scheme
(define (run-with cb x)
  (+ 1 (cb x)))               ; run-with does not name any specific verb

(define (double x) (* x 2))

(run-with double 20)
;; ⇒ 41
```

`run-with` invokes whatever it is handed. It has no static dependency on `double`; you could pass any one-argument verb. This is the maximally-decoupled shape, and it needs no import and no module boundary to achieve.

## 11.7 There is no cycle warning, because there is no cycle

The other dialect emits a warning when it detects an import cycle, because in that world load order can make a cycle work by luck and break later. Motoi has no import load order to get unlucky with. Mutual recursion between top-level verbs is defined behavior, not a fragile shape — so there is nothing to warn about and nothing to silence.

## 11.8 There is no cycle log

The other dialect logs `module-cycle-detected` events for a tech-debt audit. Motoi detects no module cycles (there are none), so it logs none. If you want to *note* a design smell your program found, Cortex is real and you write the fact yourself:

```scheme
(cortex/write 'design-note 'tightly-coupled-verbs-here)
(cortex/recall 'design-note)
```

## 11.9 Try it

Write `even?`/`odd?` as in section 11.2 and confirm they run with no cycle warning — because there is no cycle. Then nest a mutually-recursive pair inside one verb (section 11.4) and confirm that works too. Finally, pass a verb as a callback (section 11.6) and see how it decouples the caller from any specific callee. None of it needed `import`, because Motoi has none.

Chapter 12 covers identity in the other dialect — what makes two modules \"the same\" — and, for Motoi, what identity means with `eq?`/`equal?`. Turn the page.")
